{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794478275,"nonce":"TqoNbPKzJa3CFiwPA5vHQEh4dfc=","title":"Bind C and C++ libraries through WebIDL generated from their headers","message":"Decided with the user on 2026-11-12: WebIDL is for C and C++ libraries, not Rust ones. A Rust library's API is read from its `#[hxn::*]` attributes, so the Rust-to-WebIDL emitter (`hxn_gen::webidl`, the `\u003clib\u003e.hxn.webidl` that `hxn build` and `hxn package` write) is to be removed. The JSON manifest stays the one description of a Rust library's ABI.\n\n\nC/C++ header -\u003e WebIDL -\u003e hxn-gen's model -\u003e the same emitters and Haxe classes Rust libraries get.\n\n1. **Header to WebIDL.** Parse a header (libclang, or `bindgen`'s parser) and write WebIDL: functions, structs, enums, opaque handle types, callbacks. Where the header cannot say what a binding needs, write a default and mark it for review.\n2. **The WebIDL is edited and committed.** It is where what a header does not express is added, as extended attributes: who owns a returned pointer and which function frees it, whether a pointer is nullable, an out-parameter, or an array with its length in another parameter, whether a callback is called during the call or kept, whether a function blocks, which error convention a function uses (return code, errno, out-error).\n3. **WebIDL to the model.** Read the WebIDL into `hxn_gen::Library` (weedle2 already parses it in hxn-gen's tests), so the Haxe classes, the HashLink and hxcpp sides, the manifest and `hxn package` are reused as they are.\n4. **An adapter that calls C.** The Rust adapter calls the library's Rust functions; here the adapter calls the C functions instead. Either generate a small Rust adapter over `extern \"C\"` declarations, which keeps hxn-abi's handles, status and failure handling unchanged, or generate C directly. The Rust one reuses the most.\n\n\n- libclang or bindgen for step 1, and how C++ (classes, overloads, templates, exceptions) is cut down to what crosses.\n- The extended-attribute dialect. x-idl (`~/Vibranium/x-idl`) also reads WebIDL with weedle2 and has backends hxn lacks (Node/TypeScript, browser, Rayzor, Caribou); one dialect for both would let a header feed both toolchains.\n- What of the removed emitter is kept as a reference for that dialect: its mapping table (sequence, record, Promise, `[Throws=E]`, tagged enums as tagged dictionaries) is a starting point.\n- How a C library is built and linked for each runtime: as an archive linked into the adapter, or a shared library the adapter loads.","files":null}]}